{"qid":"Q887803","names":["Sultanate of Brunei"],"title":"Sultanate of Brunei","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Sultanate of Brunei emerged as a Muslim maritime polity on the northwest coast of Borneo, traditionally dated from the conversion of its rulers in the late fourteenth or early fifteenth century. At its sixteenth-century apex under Sultan Bolkiah (r. c. 1485–1524), Bruneian authority extended over much of coastal Borneo and into the southern Philippines, making it a significant entrepot in the spice and textile trades. European contact beginning with Magellan's fleet in 1521 and intensified Spanish and Dutch competition gradually eroded Brunei's commercial dominance and tributary network across the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries. The Anglo-Bruneian treaties of 1847 and 1888 placed Brunei under British protection and formalized the cession of Sarawak and North Borneo, reducing the sultanate to its present small enclave.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Core capital territory around Brunei Bay is well-documented from Chinese tribute records and European sources from 1521 onward; the claimed extent over coastal Borneo and the Sulu archipelago is attested mainly through Portuguese and Spanish accounts and is broadly rather than precisely bounded.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Brunei — Sultanate, Oil, Islam","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Brunei/History"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Bruneian Sultanate (1368–1888)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bruneian_Sultanate_(1368-1888)"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Bruneian Sultanate (1368-1888)","rulers":[{"qid"